bridgeless cubic graphs that are not 3-edge-colourable. Snarks are intensively studied mostly because many important conjectures and statements can be easily reduced to snarks, e.g. Four Colour Theorem, 5-flow Conjecture, Cycle Double Cover Conjecture. The circular flow number of a bridgeless cubic graph equals 3 if and only if the graph is bipartite and equals 4 if and only if the graph is non-bipartite and 3-edge-colourable [9], [1]. Thus, among cubic graphs, only snarks are of interest with respect to the circular flow number and their circular flow number is more than 4. Tutte's 5-flow Conjecture [10] asserts that the circular flow number of any bridgeless graph is at most 5. On the other hand any rational value between 4 and 5 is attainable as a circular flow number of a snark [6].\n\nThere are three widely studied infinite classes of snarks that represent various methods of constructions: Isaac's snarks, Generalised Blanuša snarks, and Goldberg snarks. For Isaacs snark {I}_{2k+1} the upper bound was proved by Steffen [9] and the corresponding lower bound was obtained by Lukoťka and Škoviera [5]. The circular flow number of Generalized Blanuša snarks is 4.5 [7]. The Goldberg snarks turned out to be the most challenging among these three classes.\n\nGoldberg snark {G}_{2k+1} is a graph with vertex set and edge set (see Fig. 1). Theorem 2.4 from [5] implies . Lukoťka [7] proved that . Recently, Goedgebeur, Mattiolo, and Mazzuoccolo [3] proved and, based on computational evidence, they conjectured that this is the correct value. In this paper we prove this conjecture.\n\nInstead of considering Goldberg snark {G}_{2k+1} we will consider Goldberg graph {H}_{2k+1} obtained by contracting all vertices in into a single vertex h of degree (see Fig. 1). We will show that and thus also , completing the bound of Goedgebeur, Mattiolo, and Mazzuoccolo.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProblem 3. *Removal lemma for Latin squares (suggested by Jan Hladký)* (https://kam.mff.cuni.cz/~kamak/static/problems/2020.pdf)\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.1 years ago; The removal lemma of Ruzsa and Szemeredi says that if the density of a fixed pattern (such as the triangle) in a graph is small then the graph can be made free of that pattern using only small changes. ... To quantify properly, the removal lemma would state that for every \\(\\varepsilon > 0\\) there exists \\(\\delta > 0\\) so that if the number of a \\(x\\)-by-\\(y\\) patterns \\(P\\) in a Latin square of order \\(n\\) is less than \\(\\delta n^{(x+y)}\\) then by using at most \\(\\varepsilon n^2\\) many changes we can turn the Latin square into a \\(P\\)-free Latin square.\nProblem 3. *Removal lemma for Latin squares (suggested by Jan Hladký)*\n\nThis is asking for a removal lemma for Latin squares. The removal lemma of Ruzsa and Szemeredi says that if the density of a fixed pattern (such as the triangle) in a graph is small then the graph can be made free of that pattern using only small changes. We ask whether the same phenomenon holds for Latin squares. Here, \"patterns\" are generated according to the following procedure.\n\nFix \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). Sample \\(x\\) rows and \\(y\\) columns, and view them from left to right and from top to bottom, respectively. Look at the \\(x \\cdot y\\) many values seen at the intersection and replace them with their relative rank. So, this way, provided that all the initial values seen were different (which will be the case most of the time), we get one of \\((xy)!\\) many \"patterns\". To quantify properly, the removal lemma would state that for every \\(\\varepsilon > 0\\) there exists \\(\\delta > 0\\) so that if the number of a \\(x\\)-by-\\(y\\) patterns \\(P\\) in a Latin square of order \\(n\\) is less than \\(\\delta n^{(x+y)}\\) then by using at most \\(\\varepsilon n^2\\) many changes we can turn the Latin square into a \\(P\\)-free Latin square.--------------------